      "ontology": "animals-test",
      "kind": "document",
      "text": "Field notes from the river camp. At dawn a grizzly waded into the shallows to fish for salmon while a bald eagle watched from a dead pine. Downstream the guide pointed out a croc sunning itself, and later a python slid across the trail, which sent two hikers back to camp. A ranger said that the valley also shelters a small herd of horses, but we only saw one steed tied near the gate.\n\nIn the afternoon we counted insects for the survey: a monarch butterfly, three honeybees, a dragonfly and far too many mosquito bites to tally. The children kept asking whether a big cat might come down from the ridge. The ranger laughed and said the only feline in camp was the cook's Siamese, though a stray canine sometimes raids the bins at night.\n\nBefore dinner we sent water samples to the lab in town. One student, who studies cetaceans, compared the river to the bay where she once tagged a blue whale and a pod of smaller whales. Another kept a tortoise as a child and still talks about it. Nobody mentioned the macaw that greets visitors at the ranger station, but everyone remembers its voice.",
