      "id": "music-document",
      "ontology": "music-test",
      "kind": "document",
      "text": "Liner notes. This record started as a folk project and drifted toward rock music over two years of sessions. The first side keeps an acoustic guitar, a mandolin and a fiddle at the center, with a banjo on the faster tunes and a harmonica that never made the credits.\n\nThe second side is louder. An electric guitar carries most of the melody, the drums are close and dry, and a synth fills the space where an organ might once have gone. One track borrows a reggae rhythm, another leans into heavy metal for its final minute, and the closing song is closer to soul than anything the band had tried before.\n\nThe producer kept the arrangements sparse: no strings, a single trumpet on the bridge of the title track and a tuba doubling the bass on the waltz. Several reviewers have called the record pop with country roots, and the band does not mind the label as long as people listen to the whole thing.",
